-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e91a5a3c-7b4a-5798-8092-5bb46737a70d', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Smallest Person in the Boat$q$, $q$From a column by former rowing coach Desmond Achterberg in a university athletics magazine.

Visitors to a rowing race usually watch the oars. That makes sense: eight athletes moving in unison is a beautiful thing, and the sound of blades catching the water at the same instant is one of the best sounds in sport. But if you want to understand why one boat beats another, I suggest you watch the person who is not rowing at all.

The coxswain sits at the stern, facing the crew, and holds no oar. Coxswains are usually the smallest people on the team, chosen partly because every extra pound is weight the rowers must carry. To a newcomer, the coxswain can look like a passenger with a microphone. That impression could hardly be more wrong.

During a race, the coxswain steers a boat that is longer than a city bus through a lane barely thirteen meters wide. The coxswain watches the other crews, judges when to call for a push, and decides when the rowers have something left and when they do not. Rowers face backward and cannot see the finish line. The coxswain is their eyes, and in the final two hundred meters, when every muscle in the boat is begging to quit, the coxswain is often their will as well.

I coached for nineteen years, and the best coxswain I ever worked with weighed barely more than a sack of flour and a backpack combined. She never pulled a stroke in competition. She also never lost a close race. She knew each rower's breathing well enough to hear exhaustion before they admitted it, and she knew exactly which words would bring them back.

We tend to define athletes by what their bodies can do. The coxswain asks us to widen that definition to include judgment, nerve, and the ability to make eight tired people believe they can go faster. If that is not athletic excellence, I am not sure what is.$q$, 'draft')
