-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b47fab89-20d0-5a29-9d95-b3fde7b1f6f0', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Error Messages Are Letters$q$, $q$From an opinion piece by software designer Lucia Ferreira, published in a trade journal for app developers.

Somewhere tonight, a person is trying to renew a library card, file a form, or pay a water bill, and a box will appear on the screen that says: Error 4012. Invalid input. That person will stare at it, try again, and receive the same message. Eventually they will give up, or call a help line, or ask a teenager in the house to fix it. Whoever wrote that message never meant to be rude. But the message was rude all the same.

I have designed software for fifteen years, and I have come to believe that an error message is a letter. It is written by someone who will never meet the reader, delivered at a moment when the reader is frustrated, and read with more attention than almost anything else in the program. Nobody studies a welcome screen. Everybody studies an error.

A good letter tells the reader three things: what happened, why it matters, and what to do next. Error 4012 does none of these. Compare it with a message that says: The date you entered is in the future. Please enter your date of birth as month, day, and year. The second version is longer by a few words. It is also the difference between a user who finishes the task and one who abandons it.

Developers often object that friendly messages take time to write, and that time is short. I understand; I have shipped my share of mystery codes under deadline. But consider who pays for that shortcut. The cost does not disappear. It moves from the developer, who saves ten minutes, to thousands of users, who each lose ten minutes, and to the support staff who answer their calls.

We spend enormous effort making software look polished. I am asking for something smaller and, I think, more important: that we write to our users, in their worst moment with our product, as though we respected them. Every error message is a chance to do that. Most of the time, we waste it.$q$, 'draft')
