  ('a61ff551-98c0-5de5-a66a-e54bc8ccec1e', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author writes, "Whoever wrote that message never meant to be rude. But the message was rude all the same." Why is this distinction effective for the author's audience?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It reveals that the author believes most developers secretly enjoy confusing users and only pretend that their errors are accidents.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It excuses vague messages entirely, since the developers' good intentions matter more than the users' experience of them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-viewpoint"},{"text":"It shifts blame from developers to users, who should read error messages more carefully before calling a help line.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"It separates intention from effect, so the author can criticize a common practice without attacking the developers the piece hopes to persuade.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It separates intention from effect, so the author can criticize a common practice without attacking the developers the piece hopes to persuade.$q$, $q$Readers of a developer journal are the people who write these messages, so granting that they "never meant to be rude" keeps them from becoming defensive, while "rude all the same" keeps the criticism. The author does not excuse the practice; the rest of the piece asks developers to change it.$q$, 'draft', 'b47fab89-20d0-5a29-9d95-b3fde7b1f6f0')
