-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a10f0fdd-fb91-54a2-8706-60ff47a4f21d', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When the Clocks Agreed$q$, $q$From the introduction to a local history of the Merrow Valley by historian Harriet Okafor.

The tidy version of this story goes like this. Before the railway arrived, every town in the Merrow Valley kept its own time, set by the sun at noon over the town square. Clocks in Dunmore ran four minutes ahead of clocks in Pellham, which ran six minutes ahead of clocks in Ashby. Then the railway company, tired of printing timetables that no one could follow, announced a single valley time. The towns adjusted, and the confusion ended.

Almost every sentence in that version is true. I have nonetheless come to think it misleads, because it places all the action in a company office and none of it in the towns.

The records tell a busier story. When valley time was announced, the town council of Ashby refused to adopt it for two years, arguing that the sun was a better authority than a railway. A watchmaker in Dunmore kept a clock with two minute hands, one for town time and one for railway time, and customers came from miles away to check it. The schoolmistress in Pellham taught her pupils both systems and set them problems converting between the two, which suggests that her community expected to live with both for some time.

None of these people stopped the change. That is not my point. My point is that the change was not simply handed down. It was argued over, tested, and gradually accepted by people who had their own reasons for caring what time it was. The farmer who sold milk at the station had one reason; the baker who opened at dawn had another.

I offer this history, then, not as the story of a clever decision but as the story of a long conversation. The clocks of the valley did eventually agree. It took far more people to make them agree than the tidy version admits.$q$, 'draft')
