-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cc97a657-c4a9-53ca-be12-656d8a1b34e2', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$In Defense of the Standing Dead Tree$q$, $q$From a sign written by park ecologist Tomasz Reyes and posted at the trailhead of a city nature preserve.

You may notice, as you walk this trail, a number of trees that are plainly dead. Their bark is peeling, their branches are bare, and a few are riddled with holes. Some visitors have written to us asking why we have not cleared them away. The question is fair, and you deserve a full answer.

We leave them standing on purpose. A dead tree, which ecologists call a snag, is one of the busiest places in this forest. Woodpeckers carve nesting holes in the softened wood. When the woodpeckers move on, owls, bats, and flying squirrels move into the holes they leave behind. Beetles tunnel under the bark, and the birds that eat beetles follow them. Fungi slowly break the wood down into soil that will feed the next generation of trees. A living oak supports a great deal of life. A dead one, it turns out, supports a different and surprisingly large community.

We understand that a dead tree can look like neglect. In a garden, it would be. But this preserve is not a garden. It is an attempt to let a forest behave as a forest, and forests have always contained dead wood. Removing every snag would make the trail look tidier while quietly evicting dozens of species that have nowhere else to go.

Safety matters to us as much as it matters to you. Our staff inspects every snag within reach of the trail twice a year, and any tree that leans toward the path is taken down. The snags you see have been checked.

So the next time you pass one, we invite you to stop for a minute. Look for the round holes near the top, listen for tapping, and watch the base for the pale threads of fungus. What looks like an ending is, for many creatures in this forest, a beginning.$q$, 'draft')
