-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ca2f3581-9821-590e-a114-8dc8d2fb1fb1', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Instrument Nobody Wanted$q$, $q$From a talk by music teacher Rosalind Tembo to families attending an instrument selection night at a regional youth orchestra.

Every year on this night, I watch the same thing happen. A line forms at the violin table that stretches to the door. A second line forms at the trumpet. And over in the corner, beside a bassoon that looks like a piece of plumbing someone decided to varnish, I sit and wait.

I am not here to talk anyone out of the violin. It is a glorious instrument, and some of you were born to play it. I am here to make a practical argument, and then a less practical one.

The practical argument is about arithmetic. A youth orchestra like ours needs perhaps thirty violinists and two bassoonists. Next fall, we will audition well over a hundred violinists and, if recent years are any guide, three bassoonists. A student who plays the violin competes with an army. A student who plays the bassoon is welcomed like a long-lost cousin. That student will play in the orchestra, the wind ensemble, the summer program, and, quite possibly, a few weddings, because people who need a bassoonist cannot easily find one.

The less practical argument is the one I actually care about. The instruments nobody chooses have voices nobody else can produce. Listen to the bassoon in its low register and you will hear something between a cello and a growl. Composers have given it jokes, sorrow, and some of the strangest melodies in the repertoire. A child who learns it is not settling for a leftover. That child is taking charge of a sound the orchestra would be poorer without.

So before you join the long line, I invite you to come over to my corner. Hold the bassoon. Blow into the reed and enjoy the honk, because everyone honks at first. You may find that the instrument nobody wanted is the one that wanted you.$q$, 'draft')
