  ('546006b7-4b34-5859-8439-b45890df9689', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null, 'hard',
   $q$What is the effect of the paired sentences "A student who plays the violin competes with an army. A student who plays the bassoon is welcomed like a long-lost cousin."?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The images shift the talk from orchestras to family history, which becomes the subject of the conclusion.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"The images suggest that violinists are aggressive and that bassoonists are usually related to each other.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The contrasting images turn audition numbers into a felt difference between struggle and belonging.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The images admit that bassoon players are less skilled, since they face almost no competition to join.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The contrasting images turn audition numbers into a felt difference between struggle and belonging.$q$, $q$"An army" makes the crowd of violinists feel overwhelming, while "a long-lost cousin" makes the bassoonist feel wanted, so the arithmetic gains emotional force. The figures of speech are not literal, and the speaker never suggests bassoonists are less skilled.$q$, 'draft', 'ca2f3581-9821-590e-a114-8dc8d2fb1fb1')
