-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d8a9442f-b290-54dc-a716-2fe6a10d69f9', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Art of Leaving Words Out$q$, $q$From an essay by film subtitler Kenji Aldana, written for the program of a small film festival.

When audiences praise a foreign film, they rarely mention the subtitles, and I take that as a compliment. My job is to be read and then forgotten. But I want to use this page to describe what that forgetting hides, because most viewers assume subtitling is translation with the volume turned down. It is not. It is closer to editing a poem under a stopwatch.

Here is the problem. People speak faster than other people can read. A character in an argument may say twenty words in three seconds, and a viewer can comfortably read perhaps twelve in that time while also watching faces, gestures, and scenery. Every line I write must therefore lose something. The question is never whether to cut but what.

Suppose a grandmother tells her grandson, in a long, looping sentence, that he looks thin, that he never calls, and that his cousin, by contrast, visits every Sunday. A literal translation would fill the screen and arrive after the scene has moved on. I might write: You're too thin. Your cousin visits. The grandmother's affection, her complaint, and the sting of the comparison all survive. Her particular rhythm does not, and I grieve it a little every time.

These choices are interpretations, and interpretations can be wrong. That is why I watch every scene several times, why I ask directors what a line is meant to do, and why I sometimes lie awake over a single joke. A pun that works in one language can become nonsense in another, and I must decide whether to explain it, replace it, or let it go.

I am not asking you to watch the subtitles instead of the film. I am asking you, just once, to notice that someone chose every word you read, and that the film you loved was, in a small but real way, also written for you.$q$, 'draft')
