-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c160e63c-3499-53c0-b8dc-ad7ea859f370', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Column Seven$q$, $q$The county historical society kept its oldest records in a basement room that smelled of dust and lemon polish. Priya Ramaswamy had volunteered there every Thursday since September, typing the daily weather ledgers of a canal lockkeeper named Abel Thorne into a spreadsheet for the state climate office.

For weeks the work was steady and dull. Thorne recorded the same seven things every morning: date, temperature, wind, sky, rain, river height, and a final column headed Remarks. In the first year the remarks were almost empty. "Fair." "Barge late." "Ice on the upper gate." Priya typed them without thinking and went home.

By the third ledger, she noticed the remarks were growing. "Heron back on the north post, same bird I believe." "Lent my spare lantern to the Delacroix boy, who reads by it." "River smells of rain before the rain arrives; I am learning to trust my nose more than the glass." She began to look forward to column seven the way she looked forward to the last page of a chapter.

Then, in April of 1893, the entries stopped. Eleven rows were blank. Priya checked the binding to see if pages had been torn out, but the paper was whole. Thorne had simply drawn a line through each empty day, neatly, with a ruler.

On the twelfth day the handwriting returned, smaller and shakier than before. Every number was filled in. In the Remarks column he had written: "Resumed. Water rose over the towpath on the night of the ninth. All on the east bank accounted for. The Delacroix boy carried the lantern ahead of us."

Priya sat back in the hard chair. The climate office had asked only for numbers, and the eleven blank rows would show up in their charts as missing data, a gap in a long and useful record. She opened the spreadsheet's comment field for the first blank date and began to type, slowly, everything column seven had told her.$q$, 'draft')
