-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('12e195df-ecf2-5fac-bb69-36e63ae20eaa', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Four Seconds$q$, $q$10:53 p.m.

The line on the laptop screen dropped like a stair step, stayed flat for four seconds, and climbed back up. Nadia Okafor did not cheer. She was afraid that if she moved, the line would decide it had been a mistake.

6:40 p.m.

The forecast had shown a wall of cloud moving in from the west, and Joaquin Reyes wanted to cancel. "The asteroid will pass in front of that star whether we watch or not," he said, folding the tripod legs. "We can read about it next week."

Nadia unfolded them again. Their astronomy club had spent two months preparing: building the timing box, testing the camera, checking the clock against a radio signal until it was correct to a hundredth of a second. Somewhere across the state, four other teams had set up along the predicted path. Each would measure a slightly different slice of the asteroid's shadow, and together the slices would trace its shape.

"If even one team gets clouds," she said, "the shape has a hole in it."

Joaquin did not argue, but he stayed in the car with the heater running for the next three hours.

10:49 p.m.

A gap opened in the clouds, ragged and narrow, directly over the field. Nadia called his name once. He came running with his scarf half wrapped, and they hunched over the camera together, saying nothing, watching the faint star sit in the center of the frame.

10:53 p.m.

The line climbed back up. Joaquin let out a breath he seemed to have been holding since dinner. Then Nadia's phone buzzed with a message from the project coordinator: every other team had reported clouds. Their four seconds were the only measurement of the night.

She looked at the gap overhead, already closing, and finally let herself smile.$q$, 'draft')
