-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('46712a2a-e724-56f6-ab07-0a50ff40c324', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bottom Band$q$, $q$Nothing in the wash looked alive. The canyon floor was a pale gravel bed that held the afternoon heat like a stove, and the walls on either side rose in bands of red and cream, each one sharp enough to draw with a ruler. Ms. Halvorsen had told the class that the stripes were time: every band a different chapter, the oldest at the bottom.

Ayse Demir was assigned to the bottom.

Her partner, Callum Pryce, wanted the upper layers, where an earlier class had found a trackway of small reptile prints. He sketched quickly and talked the whole time about how the bottom band was just mud that had turned into rock. Ayse did not answer. She rarely did. In two years of earth science she had asked exactly one question, and she had written it on a sticky note and left it on the teacher's desk.

She sat with her back against the band and split thin plates of gray shale with her rock hammer, one after another, the way you might turn pages in a book you were not sure you liked. Her water bottle went warm. Callum went to see the trackway.

On the fortieth plate, she found a leaf.

It was the size of her palm, printed in rust brown, every vein as clear as the lines on her own hand. It was a fern, she was almost sure, and ferns needed shade and standing water. She looked up at the canyon, at the bare walls and the white sky, and tried to see it as a swamp: dark, dripping, crowded with green.

When Ms. Halvorsen came by, Ayse held out the plate without a word. The teacher crouched beside her for a long moment.

"Write that down," Ms. Halvorsen said. "And this time, write the question, too."$q$, 'draft')
