  ('43c16b74-1029-5a5c-a4a7-f60718536d06', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null, 'hard',
   $q$How does the author's choice to assign Ayse to the bottom band, the oldest layer that Callum dismisses as mud, shape the story's meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The bottom band proves that the oldest rocks always contain the most valuable fossils.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"The bottom band suggests that Ms. Halvorsen was punishing Ayse for not asking questions.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The overlooked layer holds the most surprising past, paralleling a quiet student whose depth others overlook.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The bottom band shows that Callum was correct and that Ayse's work was mostly wasted.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The overlooked layer holds the most surprising past, paralleling a quiet student whose depth others overlook.$q$, $q$The "just mud" layer turns out to hold a swamp's history, much as silent Ayse holds an unspoken question, so setting and character mirror each other. The story makes no claim that the oldest rocks always hold the best fossils.$q$, 'draft', '46712a2a-e724-56f6-ab07-0a50ff40c324')
