-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a0b3406e-1f54-5269-ae36-e6c1b5421933', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Mapmaker and the River$q$, $q$In a valley of reeds there lived a mapmaker named Orsolya, who was famous for maps so exact that travelers said they could smell the pine trees on them. Her finest work was a map of the river Vell, which she had drawn over a single summer, measuring every bend with a knotted cord. She hung it in the village hall and refused all payment, saying only that the river should be known as it truly was.

The river, however, was not finished. Each spring the snowmelt came down from the mountains, and each spring the river leaned a little farther east, cutting into one bank and laying silt on the other. Within five years the ford marked on Orsolya's map stood in water to a horse's chest.

A merchant's cart overturned there. A shepherd lost three sheep. The villagers came to Orsolya, politely at first, and asked her to draw the river again.

"The map is correct," she said. "It is the river that is wrong."

She went to the bank each morning and drove stakes into the mud where the old channel had been, as if the water might be persuaded to return. The villagers began to cross at a new place, a gravel bar a quarter mile downstream, and to give directions by landmarks instead of by her map.

In the tenth spring, Orsolya's own grandson, carrying bread to the far bank, followed the map out of loyalty and stepped into the old ford. A farmer pulled him out by his collar, soaked and furious.

That evening Orsolya took the map down from the hall. She did not burn it. She folded it carefully, wrote the year on the back, and placed it in a chest. Then she bought new paper and a longer cord.

Ever after, the maps of that valley were dated in the corner, and travelers learned to ask not where the river was, but when.$q$, 'draft')
