-- Passage rg12-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f97f9e7c-d645-5461-8e9a-6c7bef3b8b24', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the Weather (1880s)$q$, $q$Text 1: From the Rainfall Book of Lucinda Abara (a diary, 1886)

March 3, 1886. Father signed us up as volunteer observers for the weather service, and the brass rain gauge arrived today with a ledger and a page of instructions. I am to read the gauge every morning at seven, record the depth to the hundredth of an inch, and mail the sheet at the end of each month. Father says I have the steadiest hand in the house, which I suspect means the most patience.

April 19, 1886. Forty-seven mornings. Most entries say 0.00. I have begun to wonder whether anyone in the city reads these numbers or whether they go straight into a drawer.

June 30, 1886. A letter came from the weather office today, addressed to me. They combined reports from more than two hundred stations into a map of the spring rainfall, and our county appears as a pale strip where the rain thinned out. My zeros helped draw that strip. I had thought an empty column meant nothing happened. I see now that nothing happening, written down faithfully, is also a kind of news.

Text 2: Teague's Weather (a story, 1886)

Amos Teague had farmed the same hillside for fifty years and had never owned a thermometer. When his grandson Wendell asked how he knew a storm was coming, Amos pointed rather than explained: swallows skimming low over the pond, chimney smoke curling downward instead of rising, the sweet heavy smell of clover before rain.

"Those signs aren't written anywhere," Wendell said. "How do you remember them all?"

"By watching the same sky until it stops being a stranger to you," Amos said. "The sky writes slowly. You have to stay long enough to read it."

That summer Wendell sat on the porch every evening and jotted what he saw in a notebook, which amused his grandfather. By August the boy could tell, almost as well as Amos, which clouds meant business and which were only passing through.$q$, 'draft')
