-- Passage rg12-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('23ab564f-f711-5f7d-9ead-b0371f0f5419', 'rg12-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Goats of Sarnet Island$q$, $q$Sailors left a few goats on the fictional Sarnet Island in the 1790s so that future crews stranded there would have food. No crew ever needed them, but the goats thrived. With no predators and a mild climate, the herd grew to several thousand animals, and over the next century they ate nearly every low shrub and seedling on the island.

The loss of plants set off a chain of changes. Without roots to hold it, the thin soil on Sarnet's slopes washed away in winter storms, exposing bare rock. The burrowing seabirds that had nested in that soil, small gray petrels that dig tunnels to protect their eggs, had nowhere left to dig. By 1950, surveyors counted fewer than two hundred nesting pairs on hillsides that older records described as riddled with burrows.

In 1987, an ecologist named Priya Ramaswami proposed removing the goats. Her argument rested on the link between the animals and the soil: as long as the goats kept the slopes bare, she wrote, no effort to protect the petrels directly could succeed. The goats were captured and moved to farms on the mainland over four years, and the last one left the island in 1991.

Recovery did not follow the order many expected. Ramaswami's team had predicted that native shrubs would return first, but the first plants to spread were tough grasses whose seeds had survived in cracks in the rock. The grasses were not the petrels' preferred habitat, yet their dense roots trapped windblown dust and bird droppings, slowly building new soil. Only on that new soil could the native shrubs take hold, about ten years after the goats were gone.

The petrels came back last. In 2009, a volunteer named Kofi Mensah found the first fresh burrows on a slope where shrubs had grown thick enough to shade the ground and keep it moist. Today the island holds more than four thousand nesting pairs, and the birds' droppings feed the plants that shelter them, a loop that the goats had broken for nearly two centuries.$q$, 'draft')
