-- Passage rg12-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8639ec72-9f4f-5284-944c-db11d2789b21', 'rg12-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Every Rally Counts$q$, $q$For its first forty years, the fictional Halloway Coast Volleyball League used side-out scoring: a team could win a point only while it was serving. If the receiving team won a rally, it earned nothing but the right to serve. Evenly matched teams could trade the serve back and forth for many minutes without either one scoring, and some matches lasted more than three hours.

The length of matches became a pressing problem when a regional television station offered to broadcast the league's games in 1998. The station's producer, Dmitri Kostova, explained that he could not sell advertising for a program whose length no one could predict. The league's commissioner, Abena Larkspur, asked the coaches to consider rally scoring, in which every rally produces a point no matter which team served.

Many coaches resisted. Under the old system, a strong defense could keep an opponent from scoring almost indefinitely, and teams built around patient defense feared the change would favor teams with powerful hitters. Larkspur proposed a one-season trial, and the coaches agreed, partly because the television contract would pay each club more than its ticket sales did.

The trial confirmed both the hopes and the fears. Matches settled into a predictable ninety minutes, and the broadcasts drew steady audiences. But because every error now gave the opponent a point, risky serves became costly, and teams began choosing safer ones. Defense still mattered, yet a long defensive stand no longer held the opponent scoreless; it simply decided one point.

Over the next decade, the change reshaped how players trained. Coaches spent less time on endurance drills designed for three-hour matches and more on serving accuracy, since a missed serve was now a lost point rather than a lost turn. A new specialist position, a defensive player allowed to substitute freely, was added in 2003 partly to restore some of the value that defense had lost. Clubs that had once recruited mainly for height began scouting for steadiness under pressure, and the league's youth programs rewrote their training plans to match.$q$, 'draft')
