   $q$Why did the older microscopes show a fringe of color around objects?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A simple lens bends red and blue light by different amounts.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Two lenses of different glass bent the colors apart.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"The glass was too thick to let enough light reach the eye.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Physicians placed their samples too close to the lens.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A simple lens bends red and blue light by different amounts.$q$, $q$Paragraph 1 states that "a simple lens bends red and blue light by different amounts." Pairing two kinds of glass was Oyelaran's solution, not the cause of the problem.$q$, 'draft', 'b1e57b45-2b05-5e04-9c14-814b4e94f87e')
