-- Passage rg12-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0acb84ac-43f1-5ebf-873a-03ee0d85f5f5', 'rg12-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Ponds That Need to Dry$q$, $q$Between 2004 and 2010, the number of green tree frogs breeding in the ponds of the fictional Halden Valley fell by about two thirds. A biologist named Ifeoma Castellanos, who had counted the frogs every spring for a decade, first suspected a skin fungus that had harmed frogs in other regions. She tested swabs from more than three hundred frogs, but only a handful carried the fungus, far too few to explain the decline.

Castellanos then turned to her own records. She noticed that the decline was steepest in ponds near the valley's new orchards, which had been planted on former pasture beginning in 2003. A graduate student on her team, Bao Nguyen, suggested that the orchards might be changing the water rather than harming the frogs directly. Orchards are watered heavily in early summer, and Nguyen found that the runoff raised pond levels in June and kept the ponds from drying out in August, as they always had before.

At first this seemed like good news for frogs, which need water to breed. But the ponds' seasonal drying had done something important: it killed the fish that washed into the ponds during spring floods. In permanent ponds, small fish survived the summer, and fish eat tadpoles. When Nguyen netted the orchard ponds, he found fish in nine of eleven; in ponds far from the orchards, which still dried each summer, he found none.

To test the idea, the team worked with two orchard owners to redirect runoff away from three ponds. Those ponds dried out the following August, the fish disappeared, and within three years the number of breeding frogs had nearly returned to its earlier level. Ponds that still received runoff showed no recovery.

The finding changed how the valley's conservation office manages its wetlands. Instead of trying to keep every pond full, the office now protects the natural cycle of filling and drying, and new orchards must route their runoff into drainage channels rather than into ponds.$q$, 'draft')
