-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b5daaae5-9194-5836-95e3-35c06ad73cef', 'rg12-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Messages From Very Far Away$q$, $q$When a spacecraft billions of kilometers from Earth sends home a photograph, the signal that reaches us is astonishingly weak. Radio waves spread out as they travel, and over such enormous distances they attenuate until the power arriving at a receiving dish is a tiny fraction of the energy used by a household light bulb. Engineers must somehow pull a clear picture out of a whisper.

Weakness is not the only problem. Along the way, stray radiation and electrical noise inside the receiver can flip individual bits, turning a one into a zero or the reverse. A few flipped bits might degrade a photograph only slightly, adding a speckle here or a streak there, but enough of them can reduce an image to meaningless static.

The solution is to make the message redundant. In everyday speech, a redundant word is useless because it repeats what has already been said. In communication engineering, though, redundancy is deliberate and valuable: the spacecraft adds extra bits that repeat information about the original data in carefully calculated patterns. If some bits are damaged on the journey, the receiver can compare them against the extra bits and work out what the originals must have been.

These methods give the transmissions remarkable fidelity. Images assembled on Earth match what the spacecraft's camera captured almost exactly, down to the faint texture of distant rings and moons.

Engineers describe a well-designed code as robust: it continues to work even when conditions are poor, absorbing a large number of errors before the message fails. The trade-off is that every extra bit takes time to send. Designers must therefore balance protection against speed, adding enough redundancy to survive the trip but not so much that a single image takes weeks to arrive.$q$, 'draft')
