-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fecbe175-7fa7-5b78-9537-bfdb8012095e', 'rg12-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Brainless Problem Solver$q$, $q$Slime molds are among the oddest organisms in the forest. Despite their name, they are not true molds, and for much of their lives they are not even easy to find. One common species spends its days as a bright yellow film spreading across damp logs and fallen leaves, searching for bacteria and fungal spores to eat.

A slime mold has no brain and no nerves. Its body is little more than a single enormous cell containing many nuclei. With such a rudimentary structure, it seems incapable of anything resembling intelligence; it lacks even the simplest organs that animals use to sense and respond to the world.

Yet experiments suggest that this humble organism can solve problems. When researchers placed oat flakes at the ends of a small maze, the slime mold first spread through every corridor. Gradually it withdrew from the dead ends, leaving a single thick tube along the shortest route between the food. Other scientists arranged oat flakes in a pattern matching the cities around a large urban region. The network the slime mold grew to connect them resembled, in several respects, the rail lines that engineers had designed for the same area.

How does a creature without a brain manage this? Researchers believe the answer lies in feedback. Tubes that carry more nutrients thicken, while tubes that carry little gradually shrink and disappear. The organism's choices are therefore provisional, constantly revised as new information flows through its body.

In some related species, the most surprising behavior comes when food runs out. Thousands of separate cells, which had been living independently, begin to aggregate, streaming toward one another until they form a single mass that can move as a unit and eventually release spores.

The prodigious abilities of such simple organisms have led some scientists to reconsider what counts as decision making. Others urge caution, arguing that efficient growth is not the same as thought.$q$, 'draft')
