   $q$According to the passage, why did the meadow behind the Harrowford gates sink over time?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Cattle grazing on the meadow packed the soil down until it settled below the level of the sea.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"With the tides blocked, old roots decayed and no new sediment was carried in to build the soil.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Rising sea levels during the century gradually lifted the marsh on the seaward side.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"Fresh water draining through the gates washed the meadow's soil out into the open sea.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$With the tides blocked, old roots decayed and no new sediment was carried in to build the soil.$q$, $q$The first paragraph explains that marsh soil comes from roots and tidal sediment, and "once the tides were blocked, the roots decayed and no new sediment arrived." Grazing is mentioned only as the farmers' purpose, never as a cause of sinking.$q$, 'draft', '3ff382a5-a9f8-573d-b837-4003a3af3d49')
