-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4cfa60aa-20f5-52a5-b933-c08bbfd1bf26', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What the Toll Clerks Wrote Down$q$, $q$The Ashcombe Canal ran forty-one kilometers between the market town of Marrowby and the river port of Kestlehithe, and for nearly three decades every boat that passed its toll house had its cargo written into a ledger. Toll clerks recorded the boat's name, the type of cargo, its weight and the fee charged, because the canal company set its tolls by the ton and by the kind of goods carried. Seventeen of those ledgers survive in the Marrowby town archive, covering the years 1826 to 1853 with a gap of four years in the 1830s.

Historian Ines Barraclough spent two years transcribing the ledgers into a database. Her work shows that coal made up more than two thirds of the canal's tonnage in its first decade, moving inland from the port to the town's foundries and homes. By the 1840s, however, the share of coal had fallen to under half, while the tonnage of bricks, lime and roofing slate had risen almost fivefold. Barraclough connects this shift to Marrowby's growth: the town's population doubled between 1831 and 1851, and many of its surviving row houses date from those years.

The ledgers end abruptly in 1853, two years after a railway line reached Marrowby. The final volume shows tolls falling month after month until the entries simply stop.

Barraclough is careful about what the ledgers cannot show. Clerks recorded only what boat crews declared, and fines listed in a separate company minute book suggest that some crews understated their loads to pay less. The missing years in the 1830s may also hide changes she cannot trace. Still, she argues that no other source records the town's everyday trade in such steady detail.$q$, 'draft')
