  ('984f6948-357e-54c8-98c1-982bd1693dfb', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Which detail best explains why a few centimeters gained from wind can matter in regional results?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Wind shifts from moment to moment during the course of an event.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"The gap between first and fourth place is often under ten centimeters.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Haverford adjusted her data for each jumper's personal best.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"The runway at Dunmere sits between two large grandstands.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The gap between first and fourth place is often under ten centimeters.$q$, $q$If places are often separated by less than ten centimeters, a gain of six centimeters could change the order. Shifting wind is the reason Haverford gives for not calling winners undeserving, which supports a different point.$q$, 'draft', 'd041ab23-cde4-562a-ab06-5030fa931ce9')
