-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('84a87d3c-09bb-5f91-859b-369e6b10e2d8', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Dating the Great Barn$q$, $q$In the hill farms around Aldervale, local tradition held that the great barn at Thornecastle Farm was built in the 1600s. No written record of its construction survived, so in 2019 the county heritage office asked dendrochronologist Mateo Iqbal to test the claim using the timbers themselves.

Trees in this region add one growth ring each year, and the width of each ring depends on that year's weather: wet, mild years produce wide rings, while dry or cold years produce narrow ones. Because all the trees in a region experience the same weather, they share a pattern of wide and narrow rings, much like a bar code. Over decades, researchers have built a reference sequence for the region's oak trees stretching back more than eight hundred years, pieced together from living trees, old buildings and logs preserved in bogs.

Iqbal drilled pencil-thin cores from fourteen of the barn's oak beams and matched their ring patterns against the reference sequence. Nine of the beams still had their outermost ring just beneath the bark, which records the last year the tree grew before it was cut. All nine had been felled in the winter of 1742 or the spring of 1743. Builders of that era usually worked timber soon after felling, while it was still easy to shape, so the barn was most likely raised in 1743 or shortly after.

The remaining five beams could not be dated as precisely, because their outer rings had been trimmed away when the beams were squared. Two of them, however, had no ring later than 1528, and both carry old peg holes and notches that serve no purpose in the present structure. Iqbal suggests that these beams were reused from an earlier building on the site, which may explain how the story of a much older barn began.$q$, 'draft')
