-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('26d626ff-d221-5dbe-8f99-4fd15162e07d', 'rg12-figurative-language-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Opening the Kiln$q$, $q$The ceramics room at Westbrook High smelled of wet clay and dust, and on Friday mornings it also smelled of suspense. The kiln had been firing since Wednesday, and Priya Raman had spent two days convinced that her vase was shattering into a million pieces behind the steel door.

Mr. Osei, who had taught pottery for twenty years, refused to rush. He liked to say that the kiln was the only member of the class who could not be hurried, and he treated it with the patience of a man waiting for a slow but trusted friend. When the temperature gauge finally dropped low enough, he pulled on thick gloves and unlatched the lid.

Priya had shaped the vase over three weeks. She had thrown it on the wheel, trimmed it, and brushed on a glaze that looked, in its raw state, like gray mud. Mr. Osei had warned her that glazes lie before they are fired. "What you paint on is a rumor," he had said. "The kiln tells the truth."

Now he lifted the vase into the light. The gray mud had become a deep blue, pooled darker where the glaze had run near the base. Along one side, a thin crack traced a path like a river on a map.

Priya's stomach dropped. "It's ruined."

"It's finished," Mr. Osei corrected. He turned the vase slowly in his gloved hands. "A flaw you did not plan can teach you more than a success you did. Look at where the crack starts. You left the wall too thin right there."

She looked, and he was right; she could even remember the moment her trimming tool had slipped. The crack was not a curse from the kiln. It was a sentence she had written herself, weeks ago, in a language she was only beginning to read.

Before the bell, she set the vase on the shelf by the window with the crack facing out. She wanted to see it every day.$q$, 'draft')
