  ('2055d2f0-aa5e-5493-a3a6-4fb1c02ddff6', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'easy',
   $q$The author says scientists treat lichens as "living alarm systems." What does this metaphor mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Lichens grow fastest in cities with the most polluted air.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Lichens give an early warning by disappearing when the air worsens.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lichens protect rocks from people who might try to damage them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Lichens make a loud sound when the weather turns dangerous.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Lichens give an early warning by disappearing when the air worsens.$q$, $q$The passage explains that lichens "vanish quickly when air quality drops," so their disappearance warns of pollution the way an alarm warns of danger. They make no actual sound.$q$, 'draft', '0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb')
