  ('c79e9e93-7211-5482-a559-2f52369f0948', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Some residents complained that "their noon had been stolen." Why does the author use the word "stolen"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It captures residents' sense that something of theirs was taken.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It means noon no longer happened at all in those towns.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"It shows the author agrees the zone system was a crime.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-author-and-subject"},{"text":"Thieves had taken the clocks from many town halls.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It captures residents' sense that something of theirs was taken.$q$, $q$The official midday "no longer matched the sun overhead," and "stolen" conveys how residents felt they had lost a time that belonged to their town. The author reports the complaint and later calls zones a success, so the word does not express the author's own judgment.$q$, 'draft', '5b4a954b-e234-57ca-8bbc-c282b017bb02')
