-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('86b331d4-0106-5eee-abe8-a5909b2a91ff', 'rg12-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Languages at the Counter$q$, $q$Every second Saturday, Lucia Benavides went with her grandfather to the pharmacy on Calloway Street, and every second Saturday she became, for twenty minutes, the most important person in the building.

Her grandfather had lived in the city for nine years. He could argue about soccer in three languages and bargain at the market with a speed that left vendors dizzy, but the pharmacy's vocabulary of dosages and interactions was a locked room to him. Lucia held the key, or at least she was supposed to.

This Saturday the pharmacist, a patient woman named Ms. Haddad, explained that a new prescription should be taken with food, never on an empty stomach, and not within two hours of the antacid he already used. Lucia nodded, turned to her grandfather, and froze. She understood every word Ms. Haddad had said. She did not know how to say half of them in Spanish.

"Take it with food," she began, and then hesitated over "antacid." Her grandfather watched her with the polite calm of a man who had been translated many times before.

Ms. Haddad noticed. Instead of repeating the instructions, she drew a small chart on the back of the bag: a plate, a clock, and a crossed-out bottle of the antacid. Lucia's grandfather studied it, then laughed and tapped the paper. He understood at once.

On the walk home, Lucia felt oddly deflated. "I was supposed to be the bridge," she said. "She didn't even need me."

Her grandfather shook his head. "A bridge is not the only way across a river," he said. "Sometimes the best help is a better boat. You will learn the words. Today you learned when to ask for a drawing."

Lucia thought about that all the way to their door. She had always believed that knowing two languages meant standing in the middle of every conversation. Now she suspected it sometimes meant knowing when to step aside.$q$, 'draft')
