  ('85d40b85-d22c-5fe7-909f-74dc2612c5aa', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author calls a compressed photo "an accurate lie." How does the passage resolve this paradox?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It misstates the scene's data yet matches what a viewer perceives.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The photo is accurate in color but false in its brightness.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The photo is accurate at first but becomes a lie a year later.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The photo is a lie only when the camera sensor is broken.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It misstates the scene's data yet matches what a viewer perceives.$q$, $q$The text explains that the photo "does not record what the scene actually contained" but records what a viewer "will believe the scene contained," so it is false in data yet true to perception. Brightness is what compression preserves most, so calling the brightness false gets it backward.$q$, 'draft', '823c1dda-3228-5a71-bc05-911744b562c3')
