-- Passage rg12-literary-structure-pov-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a9123e74-bca1-54be-abb7-f001a6a34f4f', 'rg12-literary-structure-pov-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bus Route 9$q$, $q$Mr. Castellanos had driven Route 9 for twenty-two years, and every morning at 7:14 he stopped at the corner of Linden and Third, though no one had boarded there since spring.

That was when Mrs. Okafor had moved to her daughter's house across the state. For eleven years she had waited at that corner with a canvas bag of library books, and for eleven years she had told him, as she climbed the steps, which one she was reading and whether it deserved her time. He had never read any of them. He had, however, developed strong opinions about several.

The transit office had sent two memos about the stop. The first suggested, politely, that drivers should not halt where no passengers were waiting. The second used the word "efficiency" four times. Mr. Castellanos had filed both memos carefully in the glove compartment, where he kept things he intended to think about later.

On a gray Tuesday in October, a teenager named Dmitri flagged the bus at Linden and Third. He had a library book under his arm and an apologetic expression, because he was not sure the bus stopped there anymore.

"It stops," said Mr. Castellanos.

Dmitri sat in the front seat. After a block he held up the book and said he was supposed to read it for class, but it seemed long.

Mr. Castellanos glanced at the cover. It was one Mrs. Okafor had carried for most of a winter. He thought about what she had said about it, which had been a great deal.

"Give it until chapter four," he said. "It improves."$q$, 'draft')
