   $q$The narrator says Mr. Castellanos "had never read any of" the books but "had, however, developed strong opinions about several." What is the effect of this contrast?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It criticizes him for judging books he has never opened, which the story treats as a serious flaw.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It gently suggests how closely he listened to Mrs. Okafor and how much her talks meant to him.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It proves that Mrs. Okafor's opinions were often wrong and that he disagreed with her.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that he secretly read every book and did not want Mrs. Okafor to know.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It gently suggests how closely he listened to Mrs. Okafor and how much her talks meant to him.$q$, $q$His opinions came entirely from her daily reports, so the dry contrast reveals affection through understatement. The tone is warm, not critical, and the text says he never read them.$q$, 'draft', 'a9123e74-bca1-54be-abb7-f001a6a34f4f')
