-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5f4b1c1d-67b8-5933-a5df-269b0018f69d', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bring Back the Benches$q$, $q$Three years ago, the town of Ashgrove removed most of the public benches along its main street. The council explained that the benches were costly to repair and repaint, and that few people seemed to use them. The decision saved a modest amount of money. I believe it was a mistake, and the town should put the benches back.

A bench is easy to overlook until you need one. For older residents, parents with small children and anyone recovering from an injury, a walk of a few blocks is possible only if there is somewhere to rest along the way. Since the benches disappeared, several neighbors of mine have told me they now drive to the pharmacy instead of walking, or they skip the trip entirely. A town that wants people to stay active should make walking easier, not harder.

Benches also support local business. A shopper who can sit for a few minutes is more likely to visit a second or third store before heading home. When the town of Merrin Falls added seating along its shopping street, store owners there reported that customers stayed longer. Ashgrove's merchants deserve the same advantage, especially as more people shop online.

Finally, benches give people a reason to linger and talk. Some of the most ordinary conversations in a town happen between strangers sharing a seat while they wait for a bus or rest in the shade. Those small exchanges help neighbors recognize one another and make a street feel welcoming rather than empty.

The council's concerns about cost are real, but they can be met. Benches made of recycled materials need less upkeep than painted wood. Local businesses or community groups could sponsor individual benches and help maintain them. The council's claim that few people used the old benches may also reflect their poor condition and awkward placement. Well-made benches in shady, useful spots would likely be used far more. Returning them would be a small investment in health, commerce and community.$q$, 'draft')
